# PROVENANCE: measured, not generated. Every response byte below was captured
# from a genuine DESFire EV3 4K over PC/SC by a PC/SC capture tool
# and is used here as an expectation for our emulator. As with the other
# hardware_physical_*.vec files, this vector is the opposite of the rest
# of the corpus: the others were recorded from the reference engine, so they
# pin what we decided; this one pins what the silicon does. A mismatch here is
# either a real divergence or a difference we can justify in writing, never a
# vector to be re-recorded from our engine.
#
# Card: UID 048222F2CF1890, hardware 51.0, software 3.0, storage 4K. Formatted
# with FormatPICC under the all-zero PICC test key, then provisioned with
# a synthetic structure whose every key is a published test key. That is what
# makes an authenticated capture safe to commit and replayable: no key material
# enters the tree, and our engine derives the same session key from the same
# pinned nonces.
#
# RndA is the fixed constant the recorder uses; RndB is the card's, recovered
# by decrypting its first authentication frame with the test key and
# pinned here with inject-random so our engine offers the same challenge.
# What this vector is for: settling the suspicion left open in
# hardware_genuine_ev1_4k_issued.vec, which could not be measured because
# the reader disconnected. That note reported our engine refusing an
# unauthenticated CreateStdDataFile in an application whose key settings
# were 0B, and read it as our engine not honouring the free-create bit.
#
# It does honour it. The bit was misidentified: 0B has bit 1 set and bit 2
# clear, and it is bit 2 that grants free create and delete. Measured on
# the card, one bit at a time:
#
#   KS1 0F -> 91 00
#   KS1 02 -> 91 AE
#   KS1 04 -> 91 00
#   KS1 01 -> 91 AE
#
# so the free-create bit is bit 2, which is what our engine already
# uses (DFC_KS1_FREE_CREATE_DELETE 0x04). An application with KS1 0B
# therefore does require an authentication to create a file, and the
# refusal the issued vector saw was correct. No engine change is needed;
# the suspicion is closed as unfounded.

name: genuine EV3 4K, application-level free file creation
case-id: hardware-physical-ev3-4k-free-create
origin: physical-card
source: captured hardware trace
uid: 048222F2CF1890
picc-key-settings-1: 0F
picc-key-settings-2: 01
picc-auth-mode: D40
picc-key-len: 16
picc-generation: EV3
picc-storage: 4096

scan
# The same CreateStdDataFile, with no session open, against four
# applications whose key settings differ only in the low nibble. Which
# bit of the nibble actually gates file creation is the question, so
# each candidate bit is isolated rather than tested inside 0F.
#
#   0F  every bit set
#   02  bit 1 alone
#   04  bit 2 alone
#   01  neither
#
# KS2 is 04 throughout, so the key type is not a variable. The file is
# the same 32-octet Standard Data file in each application.
status   905A00000300000000 => 00
status   90CA000005A3A2A10F0400 => 00
status   905A000003A3A2A100 => 00
exchange native-apdu 90CD0000070100111120000000 => 9100
status   905A00000300000000 => 00
status   90CA000005B3B2B1020400 => 00
status   905A000003B3B2B100 => 00
exchange native-apdu 90CD0000070100111120000000 => 91AE
status   905A00000300000000 => 00
status   90CA000005C3C2C1040400 => 00
status   905A000003C3C2C100 => 00
exchange native-apdu 90CD0000070100111120000000 => 9100
status   905A00000300000000 => 00
status   90CA000005D3D2D1010400 => 00
status   905A000003D3D2D100 => 00
exchange native-apdu 90CD0000070100111120000000 => 91AE
